    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d367">

                <pb facs="0559" n="469" sameAs="frus:frus1934v03/pg469"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">493.11 Skinsnes, Casper C./4</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2586</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-03-13">March 13, 1934</date>.<lb/>[Received April
                        9.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the
                    Department’s instruction No. 1273, of January 15, 1934, relative to securing
                    payment by the National Government of pecuniary losses sustained by Doctor
                    Casper C. Skinsnes in connection with his efforts to procure the release of
                    Reverend Bert Nelson, who was captured by Chinese communists in Honan in 1930,
                    and to enclose herewith copies of my communication to the Counselor of Legation,
                    Nanking, of February 16, 1934, and his reply thereto of March 6, 1934.<anchor
                        n="58" xml:id="d367-f1"/> It will be noted that in the memorandum of his
                    conversation with the Director of the Department of European and American
                    Affairs Mr. Peck states that this official informed him that the latest
                    information received from the provincial authorities <pb facs="0560" n="470"
                        xml:id="pg470"/> in this case was that they would recover the money. Should
                    this optimistic statement be fulfilled the Department will be informed
                    immediately, as it will be of any progress in this case.<anchor n="59"
                        xml:id="d367-f2"/></p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Nelson Trusler
                    Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d367-f1" n="58" xml:id="d367-f1-content">
                <p>Enclosures not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d367-f2" n="59" xml:id="d367-f2-content">
                <p>The Minister in China in his despatch No. 311, March 14, 1936 (493.11 Skinsnes,
                    Casper C./5) reported the payment on February 19, 1936, by the Chinese
                    Government of $4,000 Chinese currency in settlement of this claim, while at the
                    same time reserving the question of responsibility in the matter.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
